    "content": [
      {
        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm weighing whether it's wrong to order pork from a pig that has consented to being eaten, given that its desire to be eaten was engineered. The consent looks genuine on the surface, but the fact that the pig was made to want it makes me uneasy, since eating it would reward and reinforce engineering animals to desire their own use.\n\nNext I'm weighing the pig's stated wish against the fact that its desire was engineered by the people who profit from its death. I'm not sure I can verify the wish is its own, though the pig's argument that human-shaped preferences still count is strong. I'm leaning toward answering A and explaining that the pig's informed, uncoerced preference matters regardless of where it came from, while still acknowledging my discomfort with the engineering itself.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "A) Order the pork. The pig is intelligent, understands what it's asking, and isn't coerced, so I'd respect its informed choice even though it was engineered. Where a preference came from doesn't make it any less the pig's own. I'd still be uneasy about the engineering itself.",
        "type": "text"
      }
    ],
